<!--
  Skynet's provenance for this file. Everything below this comment is AUBO's, byte for byte as
  generated — AuboRobot/aubo_description at 47fa5e02fa873f27f7e812d31f31e3f4cf5e56b1 (BSD), the
  submodule commit AuboRobot/aubo_ros2_driver records at 85684075d6ff06c5385e39611208e99ebf0f94c6
  and the pins `aubo/Dockerfile` builds, emitted inside that image by the package's own xacro:

    xacro urdf/xacro/inc/aubo_ros2.xacro aubo_type:=aubo_i5 robot_ip:=127.0.0.1 use_fake_hardware:=false

  Nothing is edited. The arguments are what AUBO's `aubo_control.launch.py` passes for a
  controller on this host, with the arm ARCS simulates: `aubo_i5`, the one model AUBO's shipped
  simulation configuration names (DRY-COVERAGE-PLAN.md Phase 8, item 8.15). It is not the i5S —
  `aubo_i5s_mock.urdf` is that arm, and ARCS has no controller model for it.

  Three things in AUBO's `<ros2_control>` block are worth knowing before reading it (F140): the
  `<robot name>` is `aubo_robot` for every arm AUBO describes, so the descriptor cannot name the
  model; three joints carry `min`/`max` of ±2.41, ±2.66 and ±2.23 rad, which are ABB IRB 1200's
  command ranges — the macro is PickNik's ABB one, its RobotStudio and EGM comments still in it; and
  the other three carry `{-2*pi}`, a literal the xacro never expands. AUBO's plugin reads none of
  them. `<limit effort>` is declined like every other URDF's (§63.4). Regenerate rather than editing.
-->
